| Comfortable | $1,200,000 | $900,000 | ✅ | $4,297 |
| **Stretch (at limit)** | **$1,340,000** | **$1,005,000** | **✅ barely** | **$4,798** |
| Target | $1,400,000 | $1,050,000 | ❌ breach | $5,013 |

To proceed at $1,400,000 without breaching MSR, the couple would need to reduce the loan to ≤ $1,005,414 — i.e., put in an extra ~$44,600 on top of the standard 25% down payment.

---

### Risk Flags

🔴 **MSR breach** — $1,400,000 EC is $44,586 beyond the MSR loan ceiling. Max is ~$1,340,000.

🔴 **CPF OA unknown** — needs $280,000 combined CPF OA to fund the 20% CPF tranche. If their OA is short, cash gap widens or purchase price must fall further.

🟡 **Cash buffer is thin** — $36,400 left after upfront costs. No room for renovation, moving costs, or emergency. Should build to $80–100K before committing.

🟡 **Income at exact EC ceiling** — 12-month average must hold ≤ $16,000. Any variable pay volatility is a risk to eligibility.

🟡 **No CPF Housing Grant at $16,000 income** — confirm with HDB; if any grant applies, it improves the CPF OA position.

🟢 **Zero debts** — clean TDSR headroom ($8,800). If they later take a car loan or personal loan, it eats into affordability.

---

**Bottom line:** This couple can comfortably qualify for an EC, but not at $1,400,000 — MSR caps them at ~$1,340,000 unless they bring in an extra ~$45K to shrink the loan. Their cash position is tight; they should verify CPF OA balances before shortlisting units.


